 ## Overview
-Projection and MIP Techniques are image-order rendering methods that collapse 3D volumetric data into 2D images by aggregating scalar values along viewing rays, discarding intermediate depth information [S1, S2, S3, S33, unofficial]. Unlike standard alpha-blending which accumulates opacity and color to simulate light transport, these methods are order-independent and select or sum specific scalar statistics (maximum, minimum, sum) per pixel [S2, S31, S33, unofficial]. This family is distinct from [Direct Volume Rendering](1184-direct-volume-rendering.md) because it does not model light absorption/scattering but rather provides a quantitative or highlight-based summary of the volume's density field.
+Projection and MIP Techniques are a family of image-order volume rendering methods that replace the standard front-to-back alpha compositing of [Direct volume ray casting](19-direct-volume-ray-casting.md) with order-independent aggregation functions (maximum, minimum, or summation) along each viewing ray. Unlike standard ray casting which preserves depth cues through opacity accumulation, these techniques discard spatial depth information to emphasize specific intensity ranges, making them distinct from [Direct Volume Rendering](1184-direct-volume-rendering.md) and [Neural volume rendering](1576-neural-volume-rendering.md).
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on the ray $r(t) = \mathbf{o} + t\mathbf{d}$ defined by camera origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$ [S1, S2, S3, S33, unofficial]. The ray is intersected with the volume's axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) to determine the entry parameter $t_{start}$ and exit parameter $t_{end}$ [S1, S7, S15, S22, S33, S55, unofficial]. The segment $[t_{start}, t_{end}]$ is discretized into $N$ samples at intervals $\Delta t$ [S4, S8, S9, S16, S33, S40, unofficial]. At each sample point $\mathbf{p}_i$, the scalar volume value $V(\mathbf{p}_i)$ is reconstructed, typically using trilinear interpolation [S6, S7, S33, S40, unofficial].
