 ## Background
-Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) was introduced as a standard technique for visualizing high-contrast structures in medical imaging, particularly CT angiography [S1, S2, S4, S7, S13, S18, S19, S27, S33, unofficial]. The term "MIP_Mode" appears in early GPU raycasting implementations to distinguish this aggregation mode from standard alpha-blended raycasting [S1, unofficial]. The technique leverages the commutative nature of the maximum operation to allow for efficient hardware acceleration, such as using the `GL_EXT_blend_minmax` extension [S6, unofficial]. Forward projection and MinIP are natural extensions of this paradigm, adapting the aggregation operator for different diagnostic needs (attenuation vs. void detection) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+MIP was introduced as a standard technique for visualizing angiographic data, where the high attenuation of contrast agents makes vessels stand out against background tissue [S1, unofficial]. The term "MIP_Mode" appears in early GPU raycasting implementations to distinguish this aggregation mode from standard alpha blending [S1, unofficial]. Forward projection is rooted in the physics of X-ray imaging, where the detector records the line integral of attenuation coefficients. The projection family leverages the commutative nature of addition to simplify rendering pipelines, often used in pre-processing or for specific axis-aligned views.
 
